# Agent Instructions
Describe this project's purpose, implementation boundaries, and local rules here. Adapt this template; do not leave placeholders as active instructions.

## Workflow source
Declare the selected mode and applicable policy/adapter entrypoints here; remove the unused example when adopting this template.

- Pinned adoption: use `project-workflows/`, its complete procedures and `workflow.toml`; the parent pins a project-specific branch revision. Resolve `project.root` relative to that config's directory (normally `..`).
- Global-only: load the installed workflow skills by name. If this project uses a root `workflow.toml`, declare it here and use `project.root = "."`. Do not read the global installation's config as this project's config or create a submodule/branch.

In either mode read the project's declared policies and local adapters before shared defaults. Record actual override owners rather than copying their bodies here. Report unavailable required skills or context. General/service skills remain optional unless adopted. Existing pinned projects keep their branches and pins; the canonical maintenance installation uses shared main. Update the selected mode under `update-project-workflows`; publication remains separate.

## Documentation
README explains the project and how to start. TODO indexes active work. Current policies define standing guidance; spike pairs hold scoped reasoning and work state; scratch is provisional; archives preserve history. Actual project documents live in this project, outside any workflow collection. Use `capture-for-later` for individual scratch notes when saving a possibility or deliberately parking a subject. A new note uses only `status: captured` or `status: parked` unless local conventions differ; one sentence is enough. Keep known reasoning and optional revisit conditions without inventing them. Capture does not create tasks or a spike; `run-project-spike` runs active bounded workstreams. Preserve existing notes and legacy lists rather than migrating them during setup.

## Contribution coordination
Declare the project's chosen coordination route and existing review records here. Absent or disabled coordination uses local project records without hosted work items, service setup or publication. If a forge is selected, identify its target repository and platform tooling setup/use source/version; keep credentials with that tooling owner. Use `coordinate-project-contribution` from the selected workflow source. Each spike belongs to one agent/thread; independent agents use separate spikes linked to the shared outcome. Humans mediate collaboration agreements. A branch may host many spikes/issues and several eventual change requests, with Commit Work keeping atomic scope traceable. Personal editor/agent choices remain optional, and unsupported agent operations can be handed to a human. Do not infer authority to post, assign, publish or integrate from installed tools.

## Validation and Git
Record the actual validation commands and commit/review policy. Preserve concurrent work. Review scoped diffs before committing; never include private configuration or credentials. Publishing and remote actions follow the user's authorization and repository policy.

## Markdown style
Keep each prose paragraph or comment paragraph on one line and let editors wrap it. Avoid reflow-only edits. Preserve meaningful line breaks, existing local conventions, and explicit formatter requirements.
